    {
      "id": "HgB7yIU6hkPgF6YMpXYoS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -955.5268342781925,
      "y": 1113.5652957974153,
      "width": 590.7096561416071,
      "height": 10881,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ae",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 510756333,
      "version": 70,
      "versionNonce": 1210469123,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1776648453190,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Core domains\nTenant / organization management\nInvoice transaction management\nValidation and error management\nTransmission and delivery tracking\nAudit and observability\nIntegration and configuration\nUser / access management\n\nThat already makes you sound structured.\n\n2. Main entities you should have\nA. Organization / Tenant tables\n\nIf many companies use your platform, multi-tenancy is\nmandatory.\n\ntenants\n\nStores each customer organization.\n\nKey fields:\n\ntenant_id\ntenant_name\ntax_registration_number\ncountry\nindustry\nstatus\ncreated_at\nupdated_at\ntenant_entities\n\nIf one customer has multiple legal entities.\n\nKey fields:\n\nentity_id\ntenant_id\nentity_name\nVAT_number\ncountry_code\naddress\nstatus\nB. Customer system / integration tables\n\nTracks how each tenant connects.\n\nintegration_endpoints\n\nKey fields:\n\nintegration_id\ntenant_id\nintegration_type (API, SFTP, PORTAL, MANAGED_SERVICE)\nendpoint_name\nauth_type\napi_key_reference / secret_reference\nstatus\ncreated_at\n\nThis supports SaaS, API, and managed service models.\n\nC. Invoice master table\n\nThis is the heart of the design.\n\ninvoices\n\nKey fields:\n\ninvoice_id\ntenant_id\nentity_id\nexternal_invoice_number\ninvoice_type\ninvoice_date\ncustomer_name\ncustomer_tax_id\ncurrency\ntotal_amount\ntax_amount\nsource_channel (API, PORTAL, BATCH)\ncurrent_status\npayload_reference\nidempotency_key\ncreated_at\nupdated_at\n\nImportant:\nDo not store the full raw payload only in this table if large.\nPrefer storing raw payload in object storage and keep reference\nhere.\n\nD. Invoice line items\n\nIf invoice item detail is needed.\n\ninvoice_line_items\n\nKey fields:\n\nline_item_id\ninvoice_id\nitem_code\ndescription\nquantity\nunit_price\ntax_rate\nline_total\n\nIf interview is high-level, you can mention this as optional\ndepending on reporting and compliance needs.\n\nE. Invoice status history\n\nVery important. Never keep only current status.\n\ninvoice_status_history\n\nKey fields:\n\nstatus_history_id\ninvoice_id\nprevious_status\nnew_status\nchanged_by_system\nreason_code\ncomments\nevent_timestamp\n\nExample statuses:\n\nRECEIVED\nVALIDATING\nVALIDATED\nREJECTED\nTRANSFORMED\nQUEUED_FOR_TRANSMISSION\nSENT_TO_AUTHORITY\nDELIVERED\nFAILED\n\nThis is critical for traceability.\n\nF. Validation result tables\n\nTracks why invoice passed or failed.\n\nvalidation_results\n\nKey fields:\n\nvalidation_id\ninvoice_id\nvalidation_stage\nvalidation_status\nruleset_version\nvalidated_at\nvalidation_errors\n\nKey fields:\n\nerror_id\nvalidation_id\ninvoice_id\nerror_code\nerror_message\nfield_name\nseverity\ncreated_at\n\nThis separation is useful because one invoice can have multiple\nvalidation errors.\n\nG. Transformation tracking\n\nIf invoice format needs conversion.\n\ntransformation_logs\n\nKey fields:\n\ntransformation_id\ninvoice_id\nsource_format\ntarget_format\ntransformation_status\ntransformed_payload_reference\ncreated_at\nH. Transmission / Peppol / authority tracking\n\nTracks what happened after validation.\n\ntransmissions\n\nKey fields:\n\ntransmission_id\ninvoice_id\ndestination_type (PEPPOL, FTA, CUSTOMER)\ntransmission_status\nrequest_reference\nresponse_reference\nsent_at\nacknowledged_at\nretry_count\n\nThis is essential because transmission can fail independently of\nvalidation.\n\nI. Notification / webhook delivery\n\nIf clients receive callbacks.\n\nnotifications\n\nKey fields:\n\nnotification_id\ninvoice_id\ntenant_id\nnotification_type (WEBHOOK, EMAIL, SMS)\ntarget_endpoint\ndelivery_status\nretry_count\nsent_at\nacknowledged_at\nJ. Audit trail\n\nA regulated platform must have full auditability.\n\naudit_logs\n\nKey fields:\n\naudit_id\ntenant_id\ninvoice_id\naction_type\nactor_type (SYSTEM, USER, ADMIN)\nactor_id\naction_details\nip_address\ntimestamp\n\nThis is non-negotiable in compliance systems.\n\nK. Users and RBAC\n\nFor portal/admin use.\n\nusers\nuser_id\ntenant_id\nname\nemail\nstatus\ncreated_at\nroles\nrole_id\nrole_name\nuser_roles\nuser_id\nrole_id\n\nTypical roles:\n\ntenant_admin\nfinance_user\ncompliance_officer\nsupport_admin\nsystem_admin\n3. High-level relationships\n\nYour main relationships will be:\n\none tenant → many tenant_entities\none tenant → many invoices\none invoice → many invoice_line_items\none invoice → many invoice_status_history\none invoice → many validation_errors\none invoice → many transmissions\none invoice → many notifications\none tenant → many users\n\nThis is enough for architecture presentation.\n\n4. Recommended database approach\n\nDo not force everything into one database style.\n\nA better architecture view is:\n\nRelational database\n\nUse for:\n\ntenants\ninvoices\nstatus history\nvalidation metadata\ntransmission tracking\nusers / RBAC\n\nGood choices:\n\nPostgreSQL\nAzure SQL\nSQL Server\n\nBecause these are structured, transactional, and relational.\n\nObject storage\n\nUse for:\n\nraw invoice payloads\ntransformed XML / JSON\nsigned documents\narchived files\n\nStore only references in DB.\n\nOptional NoSQL / analytics store\n\nIf high search or large-volume event logs are needed:\n\naudit search\noperational dashboards\nlog analytics\n5. Important design principles you should mention\nMulti-tenancy\n\nEvery business record must be tenant-aware.\n\nUse:\n\ntenant_id in core tables\nrow-level isolation or schema isolation depending on\nscale/security\nIdempotency\n\nDuplicate invoice submission must not create duplicate records.\n\nUse:\n\nunique constraint on tenant_id + external_invoice_number\nor\ndedicated idempotency_key\nStatus-driven processing\n\nNever overwrite history silently.\n\nAlways keep:\n\ncurrent status in invoice master\ncomplete status timeline in history table\nAuditability\n\nEvery sensitive action should be traceable.\n\nRetention and archival\n\nInvoice data may need long retention.\nKeep active data in relational DB, archive old payloads to lower-\ncost storage.\n\n6. Non-functional database considerations\n\nYou should explicitly cover:\n\nindexing on invoice_id, tenant_id, status, invoice_date\npartitioning by date or tenant for large scale\nencryption at rest\nbackup and recovery\nHA/replication\nread replicas for reporting\nsoft delete vs immutable audit records\narchival policy\n\nThat makes the answer senior.\n\n7. A clean set of tables you can draw\n\nIf you want a manageable design, draw these core tables only:\n\ntenants\ntenant_entities\nintegration_endpoints\ninvoices\ninvoice_line_items\ninvoice_status_history\nvalidation_results\nvalidation_errors\ntransmissions\nnotifications\naudit_logs\nusers\nroles\nuser_roles\n\nThat is enough for an interview architecture diagram.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Core domains\nTenant / organization management\nInvoice transaction management\nValidation and error management\nTransmission and delivery tracking\nAudit and observability\nIntegration and configuration\nUser / access management\n\nThat already makes you sound structured.\n\n2. Main entities you should have\nA. Organization / Tenant tables\n\nIf many companies use your platform, multi-tenancy is mandatory.\n\ntenants\n\nStores each customer organization.\n\nKey fields:\n\ntenant_id\ntenant_name\ntax_registration_number\ncountry\nindustry\nstatus\ncreated_at\nupdated_at\ntenant_entities\n\nIf one customer has multiple legal entities.\n\nKey fields:\n\nentity_id\ntenant_id\nentity_name\nVAT_number\ncountry_code\naddress\nstatus\nB. Customer system / integration tables\n\nTracks how each tenant connects.\n\nintegration_endpoints\n\nKey fields:\n\nintegration_id\ntenant_id\nintegration_type (API, SFTP, PORTAL, MANAGED_SERVICE)\nendpoint_name\nauth_type\napi_key_reference / secret_reference\nstatus\ncreated_at\n\nThis supports SaaS, API, and managed service models.\n\nC. Invoice master table\n\nThis is the heart of the design.\n\ninvoices\n\nKey fields:\n\ninvoice_id\ntenant_id\nentity_id\nexternal_invoice_number\ninvoice_type\ninvoice_date\ncustomer_name\ncustomer_tax_id\ncurrency\ntotal_amount\ntax_amount\nsource_channel (API, PORTAL, BATCH)\ncurrent_status\npayload_reference\nidempotency_key\ncreated_at\nupdated_at\n\nImportant:\nDo not store the full raw payload only in this table if large.\nPrefer storing raw payload in object storage and keep reference here.\n\nD. Invoice line items\n\nIf invoice item detail is needed.\n\ninvoice_line_items\n\nKey fields:\n\nline_item_id\ninvoice_id\nitem_code\ndescription\nquantity\nunit_price\ntax_rate\nline_total\n\nIf interview is high-level, you can mention this as optional depending on reporting and compliance needs.\n\nE. Invoice status history\n\nVery important. Never keep only current status.\n\ninvoice_status_history\n\nKey fields:\n\nstatus_history_id\ninvoice_id\nprevious_status\nnew_status\nchanged_by_system\nreason_code\ncomments\nevent_timestamp\n\nExample statuses:\n\nRECEIVED\nVALIDATING\nVALIDATED\nREJECTED\nTRANSFORMED\nQUEUED_FOR_TRANSMISSION\nSENT_TO_AUTHORITY\nDELIVERED\nFAILED\n\nThis is critical for traceability.\n\nF. Validation result tables\n\nTracks why invoice passed or failed.\n\nvalidation_results\n\nKey fields:\n\nvalidation_id\ninvoice_id\nvalidation_stage\nvalidation_status\nruleset_version\nvalidated_at\nvalidation_errors\n\nKey fields:\n\nerror_id\nvalidation_id\ninvoice_id\nerror_code\nerror_message\nfield_name\nseverity\ncreated_at\n\nThis separation is useful because one invoice can have multiple validation errors.\n\nG. Transformation tracking\n\nIf invoice format needs conversion.\n\ntransformation_logs\n\nKey fields:\n\ntransformation_id\ninvoice_id\nsource_format\ntarget_format\ntransformation_status\ntransformed_payload_reference\ncreated_at\nH. Transmission / Peppol / authority tracking\n\nTracks what happened after validation.\n\ntransmissions\n\nKey fields:\n\ntransmission_id\ninvoice_id\ndestination_type (PEPPOL, FTA, CUSTOMER)\ntransmission_status\nrequest_reference\nresponse_reference\nsent_at\nacknowledged_at\nretry_count\n\nThis is essential because transmission can fail independently of validation.\n\nI. Notification / webhook delivery\n\nIf clients receive callbacks.\n\nnotifications\n\nKey fields:\n\nnotification_id\ninvoice_id\ntenant_id\nnotification_type (WEBHOOK, EMAIL, SMS)\ntarget_endpoint\ndelivery_status\nretry_count\nsent_at\nacknowledged_at\nJ. Audit trail\n\nA regulated platform must have full auditability.\n\naudit_logs\n\nKey fields:\n\naudit_id\ntenant_id\ninvoice_id\naction_type\nactor_type (SYSTEM, USER, ADMIN)\nactor_id\naction_details\nip_address\ntimestamp\n\nThis is non-negotiable in compliance systems.\n\nK. Users and RBAC\n\nFor portal/admin use.\n\nusers\nuser_id\ntenant_id\nname\nemail\nstatus\ncreated_at\nroles\nrole_id\nrole_name\nuser_roles\nuser_id\nrole_id\n\nTypical roles:\n\ntenant_admin\nfinance_user\ncompliance_officer\nsupport_admin\nsystem_admin\n3. High-level relationships\n\nYour main relationships will be:\n\none tenant → many tenant_entities\none tenant → many invoices\none invoice → many invoice_line_items\none invoice → many invoice_status_history\none invoice → many validation_errors\none invoice → many transmissions\none invoice → many notifications\none tenant → many users\n\nThis is enough for architecture presentation.\n\n4. Recommended database approach\n\nDo not force everything into one database style.\n\nA better architecture view is:\n\nRelational database\n\nUse for:\n\ntenants\ninvoices\nstatus history\nvalidation metadata\ntransmission tracking\nusers / RBAC\n\nGood choices:\n\nPostgreSQL\nAzure SQL\nSQL Server\n\nBecause these are structured, transactional, and relational.\n\nObject storage\n\nUse for:\n\nraw invoice payloads\ntransformed XML / JSON\nsigned documents\narchived files\n\nStore only references in DB.\n\nOptional NoSQL / analytics store\n\nIf high search or large-volume event logs are needed:\n\naudit search\noperational dashboards\nlog analytics\n5. Important design principles you should mention\nMulti-tenancy\n\nEvery business record must be tenant-aware.\n\nUse:\n\ntenant_id in core tables\nrow-level isolation or schema isolation depending on scale/security\nIdempotency\n\nDuplicate invoice submission must not create duplicate records.\n\nUse:\n\nunique constraint on tenant_id + external_invoice_number\nor\ndedicated idempotency_key\nStatus-driven processing\n\nNever overwrite history silently.\n\nAlways keep:\n\ncurrent status in invoice master\ncomplete status timeline in history table\nAuditability\n\nEvery sensitive action should be traceable.\n\nRetention and archival\n\nInvoice data may need long retention.\nKeep active data in relational DB, archive old payloads to lower-cost storage.\n\n6. Non-functional database considerations\n\nYou should explicitly cover:\n\nindexing on invoice_id, tenant_id, status, invoice_date\npartitioning by date or tenant for large scale\nencryption at rest\nbackup and recovery\nHA/replication\nread replicas for reporting\nsoft delete vs immutable audit records\narchival policy\n\nThat makes the answer senior.\n\n7. A clean set of tables you can draw\n\nIf you want a manageable design, draw these core tables only:\n\ntenants\ntenant_entities\nintegration_endpoints\ninvoices\ninvoice_line_items\ninvoice_status_history\nvalidation_results\nvalidation_errors\ntransmissions\nnotifications\naudit_logs\nusers\nroles\nuser_roles\n\nThat is enough for an interview architecture diagram.",
